== Weil Group

To generalize the local class field theory, we need the Weil group which is closely related to a Galois group, but has richer representation theory.

#definition(title: [Weil group])[
  Let $F$ be a non-archimedean local field and $F^("sep")$ a separable algebraic closure. Write $Gal_F colon.eq Gal(F^("sep") slash F)$. Let $kappa$ be the residue field of $F$, and $Gal_kappa colon.eq Gal(overline(kappa) slash kappa)$. Also $I_F$ denotes the inertial subgroup of $Gal_F$. There is an exact sequence
  $
    1 arrow I_F arrow Gal_F arrow Gal_kappa arrow 1.
  $
  Inside $Gal_kappa$ there is an element $Fr^(-1): x mapsto x^q, q = |kappa|$ called the arithmetic Frobenius, its inverse $Fr$ is called the geometric Frobenius (we also call any inverse image in $Gal_F$ an arithmetic Frobenius or geometric Frobenius). We know $Gal_kappa tilde.equiv hat(ZZ)$ is topologically generated by either $Fr^(-1)$ or $Fr$, and $chevron.l Fr^(-1) chevron.r = chevron.l Fr chevron.r tilde.equiv ZZ$ has a inverse image $W_F subset Gal_F$, we call $W_F$ the *Weil group* of $F^("sep")\/F$ and write $phi: W_F arrow Gal_F$ for the inclusion map. It is endowed with the subspace topology.
]

Since $W_F$ contains the inverse image of $1 in Gal_kappa$, it contains $I_F$, and we have a new exact sequence above the old one
#align(center)[
  #diagram(
    $
      1 edge(->) & I_K edge("d", "=") edge(->) & W_F edge("d", phi, "hook->") edge(->) & chevron.l Fr chevron.r edge(->) edge("d", "hook->") & 1 \
      1 edge(->) & I_K edge(->) & Gal_F edge(->) & Gal_kappa edge(->) & 1
    $,
  )
]

In local class field theory, an Artin map is a continuous map $Art_F: F arrow Gal_F^("ab")$ with dense image, we know that $Art_F (cal(O)_F^times) = I_F^("ab")$ and $Art_F (pi.alt)$ is an arithmetic or geometric Frobenius for any uniformizer $pi.alt$. Combined with the fact that $F^times tilde.equiv cal(O)_F dot.c pi.alt^ZZ$ we have the local class field theory stated with Weil groups.

#theorem(title: [Local class field theory])[
  The Artin map induces an isomorphism
  $
    Art_F: F^times tilde.equiv W_F^("ab").
  $
  Let $E slash F$ be a finite separable extension, we have the following commutative diagram
  #align(center)[
    #diagram(
      $
        F^times edge(Art_F, ->) edge("d", ->) & W_F^("ab") edge("d", ->) \
                      E^times edge(Art_E, ->) & W_E^("ab")
      $,
    )
  ]
  where the left vertical map is inclusion and the right vertical map is restriction.
]





== Weil-Deligne Representation

#definition(title: [Weil-Deligne representation])[
  A *(complex) Weil-Deligne* representation of $F$ is a triple $(rho, V, N)$ where $(rho, V)$ is a continuous representation of $W_F$ and $N$ is a nilpotent $CC$-linear endomorphism of $V$ such that
  $
    rho(x) N rho(x)^(-1) = q^(-v_F (x)) N, quad forall x in W_F
  $
  where $v_F$ is the map $W_F arrow chevron.l Fr chevron.r tilde.equiv ZZ$. It is said to be *Frobenius semisimple* if $(rho, V)$ is semisimple.
]

It is not obvious about the name Frobenius semisimple, but we show now that Frobenius semisimple is equivalent to $rho(Fr_F)$ being semisimple (diagonalizable). Here we take $Fr_F in W_F$ to be any lift of $Fr_kappa$.

We first show that $rho(I_F)$ is finite. As a closed subset of the compact set $Gal_F$, $I_F$ is also compact, thus $rho(I_F)$ is compact in $GL_n (CC)$. The group $GL_n (CC)$ has the "no smaller subgroup" property, namely for a sufficiently small neighborhood $U$ of $1$ there is no non-trivial subgroup: simply take any matrix with determinant $eq.not 1$, its powers have unbounded determinants. Choose such a $U$ and consider $rho^(-1)(U)$, it is an open neighborhood of $1 in I_F$, and therefore contains an open normal subgroup $N triangle.l.small I_F$ with $[I_F : N] < infinity$. Clearly $rho(N) = {1}$ and $rho(I_F)$ is generated by the finitely many representatives of $I_F slash N$.

Assume $(rho, V)$ is semisimple, it suffices to assume $rho$ itself is irreducible. The matrix $rho(Fr_F)$ acts on the finite group $rho(I_F)$, we take $m$ to be the order of this action. Now $rho(W_F)$ is generated by $rho(I_F)$ and $rho(Fr_F)$, we see that $rho(Fr_F)$ lies in the centralizer of $rho(W_F)$, i.e. it defines a representation endomorphism, and by Shur's lemma, it must be equal to some scalar $c$. Thus its minimal polynomial is $X^m - c$ which has $m$ distinct roots.

Assume $rho(Fr_F)$ is semisimple. Take $W subset V$ to be any non-trivial $W_F$-invariant subspace, we need to find a complementary subspace $W'$. For a first attempt we take $W_0$ to be the $rho(Fr_F)$ complementary of $W$, this gives a projection $pi_0: V arrow W$ such that
$
  rho(Fr_F) pi_0 rho(Fr_F)^(-1) = pi_0.
$
Now define
$
  pi = frac(1, |rho(I_F)|) sum_(sigma in rho(I_F)) sigma pi_0 sigma^(-1),
$
it is straightforward to check that $pi$ is a projection onto $W$ and its kernel is exact the complementary.


#definition(title: [Smooth representation])[
  Let $G$ be a topological group and $(rho, V)$ a complex representation, it is said to be *smooth* if the stablizer of any vector in $V$ is open in $G$.
]

Consider how a continuous 1-dimensional representations $chi: F^times arrow CC^times$ can be smooth. Since any non-zero vector spans the whole space, the stablizer must be sent to ${1} subset CC^times$, that means we need some $m >= 1$ such that $chi(x) = 1$ for all $x in U_m = 1 + frak(p)^m$ where $frak(p) = (pi.alt)$ is the maximal ideal. Take a small open neighborhood of $1$ in $CC^times$, its inverse image must contain some $U_m$, but since there is no larger subgroup than ${1}$, everything in $U_m$ must be sent to $1$. Thus every continous 1-dimentional representation is smooth.

#theorem(title: [Local Langlands correspondence for $GL_n$])[
  There is a bijection between $n$-dimensional Frobenius semisimple Weil-Deligne representations of $F$ and complex smooth irreducible $GL_n (F)$ representations. If $E slash F$ is a separable finite extension, we have the following commutative diagram
  #align(center)[
    #diagram(
      cell-size: 12mm,

      node(
        (4, 0),
        [
          $
            lr(
              \{
              #block(width: 45mm)[
                #align(center)[
                  #stack(
                    dir: ttb,
                    spacing: 3pt,
                    [$n$-dim complex],
                    [Frob-semisimple],
                    [Weil-Deligne],
                    [representations of $F$],
                  )
                ]
              ]
              \}
            )
          $
        ],
        name: <source>,
      ),

      node(
        (4, 1),
        [
          $
            lr(
              \{
              #block(width: 45mm)[
                #align(center)[
                  #stack(
                    dir: ttb,
                    spacing: 3pt,
                    [$n$-dim complex],
                    [Frob-semisimple],
                    [Weil-Deligne],
                    [representations of $E$],
                  )
                ]
              ]
              \}
            )
          $
        ],
        name: <sourceext>,
      ),

      node(
        (6, 0),
        [
          $
            lr(
              \{
              #block(width: 42mm)[
                #align(center)[
                  #stack(dir: ttb, spacing: 3pt, [complex], [smooth], [irreducible], [$"GL"_n (F)$-representations])
                ]
              ]
              \}
            )
          $
        ],
        name: <target>,
      ),

      node(
        (6, 1),
        [
          $
            lr(
              \{
              #block(width: 42mm)[
                #align(center)[
                  #stack(dir: ttb, spacing: 3pt, [complex], [smooth], [irreducible], [$"GL"_n (E)$-representations])
                ]
              ]
              \}
            )
          $
        ],
        name: <targetext>,
      ),

      edge(<source>, <target>, "->", label: [$"LL"_("GL"_n (F))$]),
      edge(<sourceext>, <targetext>, "->", label: [$"LL"_("GL"_n (E))$]),
      edge(<source>, <sourceext>, "->", label: [restriction]),
      edge(<target>, <targetext>, "->", label: [local base change]),
    )
  ]

]

We first make sense of the left vertical arrow. Given an $n$-dimentional complex Frobenius semisimple Weil-Deligne representation $(rho, V, N)$ of $F$, let $iota: W_E arrow W_F$ be the inclusion map, then we show $(rho_E colon.eq rho circle.small iota, V, N)$ is a Frobenius semisimple Weil-Deligne representation of $E$. Write $f colon.eq [kappa_E : kappa_F]$ for the inertial degree. We know that $Fr_(kappa_F)^f = Fr_(kappa_E)$, thus for $x in W_E$ the equality $q^(-v_F (x)) = q^(-f v_E (x)) = (q^f)^(-v_E (x))$ holds. If $Fr_F$ is a geometric Frobenius, we can take $Fr_E colon.eq Fr_F^f$. A power of a semisimple operator is again semisimple and we are done.

In the case $n = 1$, on the left hand side a Weil-Deligne representation is just a continuous character of $W_F$, and on the right hand side we have continuous $F^times$ characters. By local class field theory, the local base change is induced by the norm map $E^times arrow F^times$.
